#!/usr/bin/env bash
# power-failsafe -- anti-lag watchdog for this laptop.
#
# WHAT IT WATCHES -- THE CPU, AND NOTHING ELSE (2026-10-26, requested)
#   Two facts, both read fresh every sample:
#
#     1. CPU UTILISATION, from /proc/stat's aggregate "cpu " line, as the
#        delta between two samples: how much of the machine was actually
#        busy, not how much was queued.
#     2. CPU LAG. This is normally /proc/pressure/cpu "some avg10" -- the
#        share of time some task wanted a core and could not get one -- but
#        PSI alone is NOT a usable lag signal on a POWER-CAPPED machine, and
#        that is not a theory: measured 2026-10-26 at 17:20:41 the machine
#        was at 94.4% busy with all cores at 727-900 MHz under the 20 W cap,
#        and PSI read 10-12%. Every thread got scheduled; they simply ran at
#        a third of their speed, so nothing queued and PSI stayed quiet while
#        the desktop visibly lagged. A clock check is what sees that.
#
# ── WHAT COUNTS AS LAG, REBUILT 2026-11-12 ─────────────────────────────────
#
#   The old gate was `busy >= 90% AND (PSI >= 20% OR throttled-and-PSI >= 8%)`,
#   and the first half of that is why it kept missing what the owner reports:
#   the desktop crawling at 40-70% aggregate CPU. Two threads blocking on each
#   other, an Electron window, a browser compositing at 60 fps -- none of that
#   pegs 32 hardware threads, so the busy line never opened and the watchdog sat
#   silent through exactly the lag it exists for.
#
#   Lag is a STALL, so the stall is the trigger now and utilisation is only
#   context. Any one of four facts makes a sample hot:
#
#       tasks stalling   PSI cpu    some avg10 >= CPU_STALL       (12%)
#                        AND busy >= ACTIVE_BUSY                  (20%)
#       throttled work   fastest core <= CLOCK_LINE, PSI >= CPU_STALL_FLOOR
#                        (6%) and busy >= WORK_BUSY               (50%)
#       memory thrash    PSI memory full avg10 >= MEM_FULL        (8%)
#       io stall         PSI io     full avg10 >= IO_FULL         (25%)
#
#   The memory and io lines are floors over the measured idle baseline on this
#   machine -- io full avg10 sits at 2-4% for hours (Brave, the shell writing
#   state), memory full at ~0.2 -- not the 30%/50% of `some` that flapped 43
#   times in six hours in October. `full` is the honest line for those two: it
#   is the share of time that ALL tasks were stalled, which is what "everything
#   froze" means, while `some` on io counts the one process reading a file and
#   says nothing about the desktop.
#
#   The activity gate on the first fact is not decoration. PSI's avg10 is a
#   10 s average, so it LAGS the load: measured here (2026-11-12) after 96
#   burners were stopped, PSI cpu still read above 12% for about ten seconds
#   while the machine sat at 4% busy -- and the vote duly raised Performance on
#   an idle desktop. A machine at 4% busy is not stalling now, whatever the
#   average says, so the stall branch also requires real work to be happening.
#   The memory and io branches need no such gate: they are board-wide stalls,
#   and a desktop can freeze on a dying disk with the CPU nearly idle.
#
#   Utilisation alone escalates nothing, and that must stay true: a compile
#   filling every core at 100% with PSI at 3% is a machine doing work, not a
#   machine lagging (2026-11-04, three false raises in one afternoon).
#
# ── THE LADDER IS A VOTE, NOT A STREAK (2026-11-12) ────────────────────────
#
#   The sample had to be hot CONSECUTIVELY, and any cold sample reset the count.
#   Real lag is not that clean: a GC pause, a browser hiccup, one sample
#   reading 11% against a 12% line -- and the streak dies while the owner is
#   still looking at a frozen window. So the last WINDOW (8) samples are kept
#   and Performance is raised when at least HOT_MIN (8) of them were hot, at
#   least RECENT_MIN (3) of the last 3 were, and the sample in hand is hot
#   still -- a window that filled during a stall and ended one sample later
#   must not raise anything. At 3 s a sample that is ~27 s of sustained
#   stalling, tolerates the odd cold sample, and still cannot fire on a
#   single spike, because a burst never fills the window. The 5-of-8 at 2 s it
#   replaced fired ~10-16 s in, which is shorter than one page compile.
#
# ── WHEN IT BACKS OUT (owner's rule, 2026-11-21) ───────────────────────────
#
#   NEVER by itself. A raise stays until the owner picks a mode by hand --
#   Eco, Normal or Performance, whichever -- or runs `power-failsafe
#   --release`. The Eco-only stand-down used to walk a boost back to
#   power-saver 90 s after the machine went quiet, so a working session had
#   the mode changing under the owner twice per stall; that is gone.
#
#   A manual switch also cancels the watchdog's claim outright. The moment the
#   config's mode changes while we hold a boost, this backs off and leaves
#   whatever that switch did alone: no stand-down, no re-raise, no fight. The
#   watchdog never switches a mode the owner is in the middle of switching.
#
# WHY THE THROTTLE BRANCH NEEDS A STALL FLOOR (2026-11-04)
#   A slow clock under saturation is not lag, and treating it as lag is what
#   this branch did until today. Measured three times in one afternoon --
#   13:16:45, 14:30:17, 15:31:55 -- the trigger fired at 95-99% busy with the
#   fastest core at 1300-1400 MHz, PSI 3%, the GPU at 0% and the desktop not
#   lagging at all; each time it pulled Performance (120/160 W, fans up) and
#   the owner asked why. What was running was ordinary background load --
#   browser, editor, emulator, this agent -- saturating 32 threads under the
#   balanced 35/45 W cap. An all-core load clocks down there BECAUSE the cap
#   is doing its job, and with PSI at 3% nothing was waiting on a core at all.
#
#   So the clock is a THROTTLE signal and PSI is the LAG signal, and the
#   throttle branch only escalates when a stall is on top of it. The 20 W eco
#   incident this branch was built for -- 94.4% busy, PSI 10.3%, 727-900 MHz
#   -- still clears the 8% floor with room to spare, so the rescue it exists
#   for is untouched; what stops is the case where the machine is honestly
#   busy at the cap it was told to hold.
#
#   CPU_BUSY is 90%, not 98%. Measured with real work (not a `yes` burn): a
#   vitest run plus the Android emulator on this 32-thread chip peaked at
#   94.4% -- a handful of idle helper threads is enough to keep the aggregate
#   off 98 -- so a 98% line is a threshold that silently never fires.
#
# WHAT IT USED TO WATCH, AND WHY THAT WAS WRONG
#   It fired on /proc/pressure memory and io too (mem >= 30 %, io >= 50 %),
#   and on the discrete GPU being busy at all -- any draw over ~15 W or a
#   client in P0. On THIS machine that meant firing for things that are not
#   the CPU being pegged:
#
#     * io sits at 11-17 % for hours at idle (Brave reading tabs, the shell
#       writing state), so against a 12 % line the watchdog was permanently
#       in pain: 43 raises and 41 stand-downs in six hours, each pair
#       flipping the profile through power-autoswitch (two pkexec calls) and
#       firing a critical notification;
#     * four 4K NVENC streams keep the CPU idle and the GPU at P0, so a
#       video export raised the CPU and kept it raised while the card -- not
#       the CPU -- was the thing doing the work.
#
#   The owner's rule now: the CPU spiked to 100 % and started lagging, or
#   nothing. Everything else is left alone, including the GPU.
#
# WHAT IT DOES WHEN THE CPU IS PEGGED AND LAGGING -- ALARM ONLY BY DEFAULT
#   Nothing is switched, raised or unlocked. It logs the offenders and says so
#   on screen:
#     1. journal line naming the trigger and the top processes
#     2. notify-send   (a real notification)
#     3. qs -c ii ipc call failsafe flash …  (bar blink + corner card)
#
#   The power escalation this used to do -- performance profile, 120/160 W cap,
#   turbo, GPU Dynamic Boost -- is still there behind FAILSAFE_ESCALATE=1, but
#   it is OFF by default on purpose (2026-10-26, owner's rule): a 100% CPU
#   event is a thing to NAME and REMOVE, not a thing to power through. Raising
#   the ceiling turns a spike into heat, fan noise and battery drain and leaves
#   the actual cause untouched; the cause here was a leftover vitest run and a
#   headless emulator, which no amount of watts fixes.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT FIRE ON A BURST -- the fail-safe ladder, all of which must
# hold before it touches anything:
#
#   * The vote above: HOT_MIN of the last WINDOW samples hot, and
#     RECENT_MIN of the last three. It fires in ~10-16 s of sustained lag,
#     survives the odd cold sample (the old streak did not, and real lag is
#     never clean -- see "THE LADDER IS A VOTE"), and cannot fire on one
#     spike, because a burst never fills the window.
#   * A minimum COOLDOWN between raises, so a flapping machine cannot get
#     profile flips every COOLDOWN.
#   * Every decision is logged to the journal (systemd user service), so
#     "why did it fire" is always answerable.
# The two lines, calibrated on this machine (32 hardware threads, measured
# 2026-10-26 with `yes` burns while sampling both numbers):
#
#     one thread per core (32) -> busy 98-99 %, lag  2-4 %   (pegged, NO lag)
#     two per core       (64)  -> busy 99-100 %, lag  9-14 %  (queueing starts)
#     four per core      (128) -> busy 99-100 %, lag 14-31 %  (real stalling)
#     idle                     -> busy 1-3 %,   lag  0-2 %
#
# So 100 % busy on its own is NOT lag -- a compile filling every core stalls
# nobody -- and the STALL line has to sit above the one-thread-per-core case by
# enough that only genuine oversubscription trips it. 12 % is inside the 4x band
# and three times clear of the 1x band; it is a little under the old 20 because
# the old line also demanded a pegged CPU, and this one does not.
# 2026-11-21: the owner's rule is that this may fire only when the machine is
# unusable -- "if I can almost do nothing" -- never because it is merely loaded.
# These lines were 12% PSI and 20% busy, and they fired three times in one
# morning (08:24, 08:29, 08:32) on 88-99% busy at 12-18% PSI: a machine doing
# work, with a task queueing here and there, not one nobody could use. 25% PSI
# at 50% busy is the four-threads-per-core band measured further down (14-31%
# lag), i.e. real queueing that a person feels at the keyboard.
CPU_STALL=${CPU_STALL:-25}      # PSI cpu "some avg10" that counts as a stall
WORK_BUSY=${WORK_BUSY:-85}      # busy% the throttle branch needs before it speaks
# The throttle branch's own floor, so a slow clock alone cannot escalate:
# see the 2026-11-04 note in the header.
# 15, not 6: at 6 the 08:24 raise ("cores throttled, 8% PSI, 99% busy") went
# through, and 8% PSI on this chip is work being done, not a frozen desktop.
CPU_STALL_FLOOR=${CPU_STALL_FLOOR:-15}  # PSI cpu the throttle branch also needs
# Memory and io, on their `full` lines and as FLOORS over the idle baseline
# (measured idle, 2026-11-12: memory full 0.2%, io full 2-4%). A desktop that is
# merely loading a page never reaches these; a machine swapping or waiting on a
# dying disk does.
# PSI's avg10 lags the load by ~10 s, so a stall line on its own kept firing
# in that tail with the machine idle (measured 2026-11-12). The stall branch
# therefore needs this much work to be happening as well.
ACTIVE_BUSY=${ACTIVE_BUSY:-50}  # busy% required for the PSI-cpu stall branch
MEM_FULL=${MEM_FULL:-8}         # PSI memory "full avg10" that counts as thrash
IO_FULL=${IO_FULL:-25}          # PSI io "full avg10" that counts as a disk stall
# THE THROTTLE LINE IS DERIVED FROM THE CHIP, NOT HARD-CODED (2026-10-26).
#
# It was 1200 MHz, calibrated on the 20 W eco cap where the fastest core sat
# at 727-900. Measured again the same evening at 98.1% busy on the balanced
# cap: fastest core 1400 MHz, PSI 3.5% -- so BOTH lag signals read "fine" and
# the rescue could not fire at any load. 1400 MHz on an i9-14900HX (base
# 2200, turbo 5800) is the machine being held back, but it is not below a
# number that was picked for a different cap.
#
# So the line is a fraction of /sys/.../policy0/base_frequency -- "the
# fastest core cannot even reach CLOCK_PCT of its base clock" is throttling
# on any profile, and it re-calibrates itself when the cap changes. 80% of
# 2200 = 1760 MHz here. An explicit CLOCK_LINE in the environment still wins.
# 65%, not 80: 80% of 2200 is 1760 MHz, and this machine idles at 900-1400 on
# the eco cap while it is perfectly usable -- the old line was reading normal
# behaviour as throttling. 65% (1430 MHz here) held down under a heavy load is
# the "nothing answers" state this exists to rescue.
CLOCK_PCT=${CLOCK_PCT:-65}      # fastest core below this % of base clock = throttled
CLOCK_LINE=${CLOCK_LINE:-}
if [ -z "$CLOCK_LINE" ]; then
    _base=$(cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy0/base_frequency 2>/dev/null)
    case "$_base" in
        ''|*[!0-9]*) CLOCK_LINE=1200 ;;
        *) CLOCK_LINE=$(( _base / 1000 * CLOCK_PCT / 100 )) ;;
    esac
    unset _base
fi
# 2026-10-26 17:20:41, the incident all of this comes from: 94.4% busy, PSI
# 10.3%, 727-900 MHz, profile power-saver (20 W PL1, turbo off) while the AC
# was plugged in. Nothing queued -- everything just ran at ~800 MHz.
# A longer, stricter ladder (2026-11-21): 8 of 12 samples at 3 s is ~27 s of
# near-continuous hard stall before anything moves, and all of the last three
# samples must be hot when it fires, so a machine that has just recovered never
# trips it. 15 minutes between raises, because a mode change costs the owner a
# screen modeset (see the memory/disk lane note in the loop).
INTERVAL=${INTERVAL:-3}         # seconds between samples
WINDOW=${WINDOW:-12}            # samples in the vote (x INTERVAL = 36 s)
HOT_MIN=${HOT_MIN:-8}           # hot samples in that window needed to fire
RECENT_MIN=${RECENT_MIN:-3}     # of the last 3 samples, needed to fire
COOLDOWN=${COOLDOWN:-900}       # min seconds between raises
# There is no automatic stand-down any more: a raise is released by picking a
# mode by hand, or with `--release`. It never walks itself back to Eco while
# the owner is working.

# Set the moment WE raise performance; cleared when we hand it back (Eco only)
# or when the owner picks a mode while we hold it. Only our own engagement is
# ever undone by us.
engaged=0
engaged_at=0
engaged_mode=""     # the owner's mode at the instant we engaged
calm_run=0          # consecutive cold samples (logged only; no stand-down on calm)
quiet_since=0

hist=""             # the vote: one "0"/"1" per sample, newest last
last_hot=""         # for logging transitions instead of every sample
last_action=0

STATUS_ONLY=0
RELEASE_NOW=0
SELFTEST=0
for arg in "$@"; do
    case "$arg" in
        --status)   STATUS_ONLY=1 ;;
        --release)  RELEASE_NOW=1 ;;
        --selftest) SELFTEST=1 ;;
        # The help is the header comment, up to the first line of code -- the
        # same rule power-autoswitch uses, so it cannot go stale.
        -h|--help)  awk 'NR > 1 { if ($0 !~ /^#/ && $0 !~ /^$/) exit; print }' "$0"; exit 0 ;;
        *)          echo "unknown argument: $arg" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
done

# The notification icon for anything ABOUT the machine lagging.
#
# An absolute PATH, not a name: the shell draws an app icon with its icon
# resolver, which does not search ~/.local/share/icons for a bare name --
# measured 2026-11-18 -- so `-i hyprland` fell back to the generic broken-app
# picture the owner reported, while the same file by path draws the Hyprland
# mark. The file is extras/icons/hicolor/512x512/apps/hyprland.svg in the
# dotfiles repo, installed to the path below; the old "speed" here was a
# Material symbol name, which is not in any icon theme at all.
HYPR_ICON="${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/icons/hicolor/512x512/apps/hyprland.svg"

log() { printf '[power-failsafe] %s\n' "$*" >&2; }

notify() {
    # $1 = -i <icon>, $2 = title, $3 = body (getopt-free: the only caller is
    # this script, so a tiny shift-based parse is enough).
    #
    # THROUGH D-BUS, and notify-send only as a fallback. notify-send is broken
    # on this machine -- `notify-send: symbol lookup error: undefined symbol:
    # notify_notification_get_activation_app_launch_context`, measured
    # 2026-11-18 -- so every alert this daemon has ever sent through it died
    # on the spot, and a lag notice only ever reached the screen through the
    # shell's own card. The extra 2>/dev/null kept it invisible in the
    # journal. The shell's notification server is the same destination either
    # way; this just stops asking a broken client to reach it.
    local icon="$HYPR_ICON"
    if [ "$1" = "-i" ]; then icon=$2; shift 2; fi
    if command -v gdbus >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        gdbus call --session --dest org.freedesktop.Notifications \
            --object-path /org/freedesktop/Notifications \
            --method org.freedesktop.Notifications.Notify \
            "power-failsafe" 0 "$icon" "$1" "$2" "[]" "{}" 9000 \
            >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0
    fi
    command -v notify-send >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
    notify-send -u critical -a "power-failsafe" -i "$icon" "$1" "$2" 2>/dev/null || true
}

blink() {
    # $1 = flash kind ("fire" performance engaged, "green" stood down,
    # "error" generic trouble), $2 = the sentence. Best-effort: the shell may
    # be reloading or gone; never let this fail the action itself. Direct IPC
    # only -- routing through shell.sh would risk launching a whole second
    # shell just to show a blink.
    #
    # The pgrep guard is not paranoia: a `qs -c ii ipc call` client that
    # cannot immediately find the instance can fall back to SPAWNING the
    # config -- and a second full shell instance segfaults on this build
    # (2026-10-01: an orphaned backgrounded IPC client did exactly that and
    # popped the crash dialog). The shell check filters zombies: a Z-state
    # process still matches a plain pgrep. The shell must be alive AND not a
    # zombie for the blink to be worth attempting.
    # The shell process is named `qs` on this build (`qs -c ii`), NOT
    # `quickshell`. The guard only looked for the long name, so it never found
    # the running shell and every blink was skipped with "shell not alive" --
    # a notification this daemon printed while the desktop it wanted to warn
    # was plainly on screen. Both names are accepted, because either could be
    # the launcher's choice after an update.
    local alive=0 pid stat
    for pid in $(pgrep -x qs; pgrep -x quickshell); do
        stat=$(ps -o stat= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')
        # Zombies start with Z; anything else (S, R, S<, SNl, ...) is live.
        [ -n "$stat" ] && [ "${stat:0:1}" != "Z" ] && alive=1
    done
    [ "$alive" = "1" ] || { log "shell not alive — skipping blink"; return 0; }
    qs -c ii ipc call failsafe flash "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    return 0
}

# PSI "some" avg10, as a whole percent. File format is
#   some avg10=0.00 avg60=0.00 avg300=0.00 total=...
#   full avg10=...
# Split on [= ] the pair "avg10=0.00" becomes TWO fields ("avg10", "0.00"),
# so the loop matches the literal token and prints its successor -- matching
# /^avg10=/ matches nothing, and the whole detector reads 0 forever. The line
# selector is the literal "some" ($1): each /proc/pressure/<res> file only
# describes its own resource.
psi_some() {
    awk -F'[= ]' '$1 == "some" { for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == "avg10") { print $(i+1); exit } }' "/proc/pressure/$1" 2>/dev/null | cut -d. -f1
}

# The `full` line: the share of time EVERY task was stalled. For memory and io
# this is the number that means "the desktop froze"; `some` on io counts
# whichever single process is reading a file, which is why it never drops below
# a few percent on an idle machine and cannot be used as a floor.
psi_full() {
    awk -F'[= ]' '$1 == "full" { for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == "avg10") { print $(i+1); exit } }' "/proc/pressure/$1" 2>/dev/null | cut -d. -f1
}

# ── CPU utilisation, from two /proc/stat samples ────────────────────────────
# /proc/stat's first line is the aggregate:
#   cpu  user nice system idle iowait irq softirq steal guest guest_nice
# "busy" is the total minus idle AND iowait: time spent waiting on a disk is
# not the CPU being pegged (and io is no longer a trigger of its own, so
# counting it as busy would smuggle it back in).
cpu_sample() {
    awk '/^cpu / { idle = $5 + $6; total = 0; for (i = 2; i <= NF; i++) total += $i; printf "%d %d\n", idle, total }' /proc/stat
}

read -r cpu_prev_idle cpu_prev_total < <(cpu_sample)
read -r cpu_prev_idle cpu_prev_total <<<"${cpu_prev_idle:-0} ${cpu_prev_total:-0}"

# THE SAMPLE STATE MUST NOT LIVE IN A SUBSHELL (fixed 2026-10-26).
#
# This used to be `busy=$(cpu_busy)` with cpu_busy echoing its result. A
# command substitution runs the function in a SUBSHELL, so the two globals it
# assigned (cpu_prev_idle / cpu_prev_total) were thrown away every sample and
# the window never advanced: every reading was "busy since the daemon
# STARTED", not "busy in the last INTERVAL". On a daemon that had been up for
# hours that number only ever fell towards the machine's idle average -- so
# `busy -ge 90` was false during a genuine 100% spike and the whole watchdog
# was dead code. Proven by running it by hand under a 31-thread burn: 98% busy
# logged within 2 s of start (window = its whole life, all load), while the
# systemd instance logging the same burn stayed silent.
#
# So the function now sets CPU_BUSY_NOW in the CURRENT shell and is called
# without $(...). Same numbers, real window.
cpu_busy_now() {
    local idle total di dt
    read -r idle total < <(cpu_sample)
    di=$((idle - cpu_prev_idle))
    dt=$((total - cpu_prev_total))
    cpu_prev_idle=$idle
    cpu_prev_total=$total
    CPU_BUSY_NOW=0
    [ "$dt" -gt 0 ] || return
    # Guard against a counter reset / a shorter window than one tick.
    [ "$di" -ge 0 ] && [ "$di" -le "$dt" ] || return
    CPU_BUSY_NOW=$(( (100 * (dt - di)) / dt ))
}

top_offenders() {
    # Whose fault it is, measured over the last second -- NOT `ps -o pcpu`,
    # whose number is an average over the process's whole lifetime. On this
    # machine that average belongs to whatever has been running longest (the
    # Android emulator at ~53 %, the shell at ~31 %), so an alarm built on it
    # named the wrong processes while the actual spike -- five vitest workers
    # at ~95 % -- never appeared. Two /proc snapshots, one second apart.
    local n=${1:-5}
    python3 - "$n" <<'PY' 2>/dev/null
import glob, os, sys, time

def snap():
    d = {}
    for p in glob.glob("/proc/[0-9]*"):
        try:
            raw = open(p + "/stat", "rb").read().decode("utf-8", "replace")
        except OSError:
            continue
        e = raw.rfind(")")
        if e < 0:
            continue
        f = raw[e + 2:].split()
        try:
            d[p.rsplit("/", 1)[1]] = (raw[raw.find("(") + 1:e], int(f[11]) + int(f[12]))
        except (IndexError, ValueError):
            continue
    return d

a = snap()
t0 = time.time()
time.sleep(1.0)
b = snap()
elapsed = max(time.time() - t0, 0.001)
hz = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK")
rows = []
for pid, (name, ticks) in b.items():
    if pid in a:
        pct = 100.0 * (ticks - a[pid][1]) / hz / elapsed
        if pct >= 5:
            rows.append((pct, name, pid))
rows.sort(reverse=True)
print("; ".join(f"{n} {p:.0f}% (pid {i})" for p, n, i in rows[:int(sys.argv[1])]) or "nothing above 5%")
PY
}

cur_freq_mhz() {
    # The FASTEST core, because "the machine is throttled" must not be
    # claimed just because one parked core sits low. Every policy is read;
    # unreadable cpufreq means "unknown", which is reported as a high number
    # so the decision falls back to PSI instead of guessing "throttled".
    local f max=0 v
    for f in /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy*/scaling_cur_freq; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || continue
        v=$(cat "$f" 2>/dev/null) || continue
        case "$v" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
        v=$((v / 1000))
        [ "$v" -gt "$max" ] && max=$v
    done
    [ "$max" -gt 0 ] && echo "$max" || echo 99999
}

desired_mode() {
    # The user's own power mode, as the shell's config records it. This is the
    # source of truth for the CPU profile, NOT the profile PPD happens to be
    # on: the shell re-asserts this key (services/PowerMode.qml) whenever the
    # config re-signals, so any profile this daemon writes that disagrees with
    # it is put back within the minute. Reading it here is what lets the
    # failsafe raise the machine without stealing the setting.
    # FAILSAFE_MODE exists so both sides of the stand-down rule can be tested
    # without touching the owner's config, and an unknown value is ignored
    # rather than obeyed: a bad mode would silently mean "never stand down".
    case "${FAILSAFE_MODE:-}" in
        eco|default|performance) echo "$FAILSAFE_MODE"; return 0 ;;
    esac
    # One grep, not a python spawn on every sample: this is re-read each tick
    # while a boost is held, to notice the owner switching mode. The JSON parse
    # below stays as the fallback for a reformatted file.
    local mode
    mode=$(grep -o '"powerMode"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"[a-z]*"' \
        "$HOME/.config/illogical-impulse/config.json" 2>/dev/null \
        | head -1 | awk -F'"' 'NF >= 4 { print $4 }')
    case "$mode" in
        eco|default|performance) echo "$mode"; return 0 ;;
    esac
    python3 - <<'PY' 2>/dev/null || echo default
import json, os
p = os.path.expanduser("~/.config/illogical-impulse/config.json")
try:
    with open(p) as f:
        cfg = json.load(f)
except Exception:
    print("default"); raise SystemExit
print(cfg.get("battery", {}).get("powerMode", "default"))
PY
}

apply_performance() {
    # $1 = the vote that fired ("5/8"), for the record and the notification
    # $2 = busy % at the moment it fired, $3 = the reason sentence
    local vote=$1 busy=${2:-?} lag=${3:-?}
    # The user's setting wins. If the config already says performance, there is
    # nothing here to raise: the profile is theirs and the boost is on, so
    # engaging would only mean standing down later -- which is exactly the "it
    # jumped back to power saving" this used to cause.
    if [ "$(desired_mode)" = "performance" ]; then
        log "lagging at ${busy}% busy — ${lag}; the user's mode is already performance, nothing to raise"
        last_action=$(date +%s)
        return 0
    fi
    # Already raised and still holding: a long compile stays pegged for its
    # whole run, so without this the ladder would re-fire every COOLDOWN,
    # re-notifying each time about a boost that is already on. Nothing to do
    # is the correct answer here.
    if [ "${engaged:-0}" = "1" ] \
        && [ "$(powerprofilesctl get 2>/dev/null)" = "performance" ] \
        && systemctl is-active --quiet nvidia-powerd.service; then
        log "still lagging at ${busy}% busy — ${lag}; boost already on — holding"
        last_action=$(date +%s)
        return 0
    fi
    log "lagging at ${busy}% busy — ${lag} (vote ${vote}): reporting"

    # Default: name the spike, change nothing about the hardware. See the
    # header -- escalation is opt-in, so a heavy build never turns into a
    # 120/160 W, fans-up, turbo-on event behind the owner's back.
    if [ "${FAILSAFE_ESCALATE:-0}" != "1" ]; then
        local top
        top=$(top_offenders 5)
        log "alarm only, no hardware change (set FAILSAFE_ESCALATE=1 to escalate). top: ${top:-unknown}"
        notify -i "$HYPR_ICON" "CPU pegged and lagging" \
            "${busy}% busy -- ${lag}. Top: ${top:-unknown}"
        blink fire "CPU pegged — ${busy}% busy, ${lag}"
        # Remember we reported it so the same spike does not re-notify every
        # COOLDOWN while it lasts, and stay un-engaged so stand_down never
        # tries to undo a change we did not make.
        engaged=0
        quiet_since=0
        last_action=$(date +%s)
        return 0
    fi
    # DRY-RUN exists so the trigger can be PROVEN on a real spike without
    # touching the machine: engaging normally converges the CPU cap through
    # pkexec, which can raise an authentication dialog in the middle of
    # whatever the owner is doing. With FAILSAFE_DRY_RUN=1 the decision and
    # its reason are logged and nothing is written.
    if [ "${FAILSAFE_DRY_RUN:-0}" = "1" ]; then
        log "DRY-RUN would set the CPU to performance and enable GPU Dynamic Boost"
        engaged=1
        engaged_mode=$(desired_mode)
        engaged_at=$(date +%s)
        quiet_since=0
        last_action=$(date +%s)
        return 0
    fi
    powerprofilesctl set performance 2>/dev/null \
        && log "cpu profile -> performance" \
        || log "WARN: powerprofilesctl set performance failed"

    # Converge the CAP now instead of waiting for power-autoswitch's next 5 s
    # poll. Measured 2026-10-26: without this the profile was performance at
    # 17:28:56 but the package limit was still 20/30 W with turbo off until
    # 17:29:00 -- five seconds of the spike spent in the cap this rescue exists
    # to lift. Same call the shell's power-mode switch makes, so the cap keeps
    # exactly one writer and one policy table.
    if [ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/power-autoswitch" ]; then
        "$HOME/.local/bin/power-autoswitch" --converge 2>&1 | sed 's/^/[converge] /' >&2 || true
    fi
    if systemctl is-active --quiet nvidia-powerd.service; then
        log "gpu dynamic boost already active"
    else
        systemctl start nvidia-powerd.service 2>/dev/null \
            && log "gpu dynamic boost started" \
            || log "WARN: could not start nvidia-powerd (needs admin? it is boot-enabled, so this should not happen)"
    fi
    engaged=1
    engaged_mode=$(desired_mode)
    engaged_at=$(date +%s)
    quiet_since=0
    notify -i "local_fire_department" "Performance mode engaged" \
        "Lagging at ${busy}% busy (${lag}) for a ${vote} vote -- ${lag}. Switched CPU to Performance and enabled GPU Dynamic Boost."
    blink fire "Power failsafe: CPU pegged and lagging — switched to Performance"
    last_action=$(date +%s)
}

stand_down() {
    # Back out of OUR OWN performance engagement, ON REQUEST: this is
    # `power-failsafe --release` and nothing else (2026-11-21). The daemon no
    # longer calls it -- a boost raised by the ladder stays until the owner
    # picks a mode by hand (the loop notices that and honours it) or releases
    # the boost with --release.
    #
    # The profile is only ever moved back to the OWNER'S OWN mode, never to a
    # hardcoded power-saver: the shell re-asserts battery.powerMode, so writing
    # something else here is undone a minute later anyway -- and getting that
    # wrong is the whole reason this daemon once looked like it was "jumping
    # back". If the mode IS performance there is nothing of ours to undo.
    local mode target
    mode=$(desired_mode)
    log "releasing the boost by hand (owner's mode: ${mode})"
    # What it returns to is the USER'S MODE, not a hardcoded power-saver. The
    # shell re-asserts battery.powerMode, so writing power-saver here over a
    # user who picked balanced or performance is undone a minute later anyway --
    # and getting that wrong is the whole reason this daemon looked like it was
    # "jumping back". If the mode IS performance, there is nothing of ours to
    # undo and the correct move is to change nothing at all.
    case "$mode" in
        performance) target=performance ;;
        eco)         target=power-saver ;;
        *)           target=balanced ;;
    esac
    if [ "${FAILSAFE_DRY_RUN:-0}" = "1" ]; then
        log "DRY-RUN would stand down to ${target} and stop GPU Dynamic Boost"
        engaged=0
        quiet_since=0
        last_action=0
        return 0
    fi
    if [ "$target" = "performance" ]; then
        log "user mode is performance — leaving the profile and the boost as they are"
        engaged=0
        quiet_since=0
        last_action=0
        notify -i "local_fire_department" "Performance mode is yours" \
            "Boost released. Leaving the CPU profile and the GPU boost exactly as your Performance mode set them."
        blink green "Failsafe: boost released — Performance mode is yours"
        return
    fi
    if [ "$(powerprofilesctl get 2>/dev/null)" != "$target" ]; then
        powerprofilesctl set "$target" 2>/dev/null \
            && log "cpu profile -> $target (the user's mode: $mode)" \
            || log "WARN: powerprofilesctl set $target failed"
    else
        log "cpu profile already $target (the user's mode: $mode)"
    fi
    if systemctl is-active --quiet nvidia-powerd.service; then
        systemctl stop nvidia-powerd.service 2>/dev/null \
            && log "gpu dynamic boost stopped" \
            || log "WARN: could not stop nvidia-powerd"
    fi
    engaged=0
    quiet_since=0
    # A hand release is not a reason to blind the next raise: the ladder's own
    # vote is what stops a blip from escalating, and keeping last_action here
    # would leave a real stall unanswered for up to a full COOLDOWN.
    last_action=0
    notify -i "check_circle" "Boost released" \
        "Back to ${mode}."
    blink green "Failsafe: boost released — back to ${mode}"
}

# ── classifying one sample ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sets HOT (0/1) and WHY. One function, used by the daemon, by --status and by
# --selftest, so what is tested is what runs.
classify() {  # busy, psi_cpu, mem_full, io_full, freq
    local busy=$1 lag=$2 memfull=$3 iofull=$4 freq=$5
    HOT=0
    # WHICH KIND of stall this is. The ladder below only ever RAISES anything
    # for the CPU lanes; see the raise site for why memory and io are lanes of
    # their own (2026-11-17).
    LANE=none
    WHY="nothing above the lines"
    if [ "$lag" -ge "$CPU_STALL" ] && [ "$busy" -ge "$ACTIVE_BUSY" ]; then
        HOT=1; LANE=cpu; WHY="tasks stalling (${lag}% PSI cpu at ${busy}% busy)"
    elif [ "$busy" -ge "$WORK_BUSY" ] && [ "$freq" -le "$CLOCK_LINE" ] \
        && [ "$lag" -ge "$CPU_STALL_FLOOR" ]; then
        # Throttled AND stalling. The clock alone is not lag: an all-core load
        # running at the cap it was given clocks down on purpose and stalls
        # nobody (2026-11-04, see the header).
        HOT=1; LANE=cpu; WHY="cores throttled (${freq} MHz, line ${CLOCK_LINE}) with ${lag}% PSI"
    elif [ "$memfull" -ge "$MEM_FULL" ]; then
        HOT=1; LANE=mem; WHY="memory reclaim stalling everything (${memfull}% PSI mem full)"
    elif [ "$iofull" -ge "$IO_FULL" ]; then
        HOT=1; LANE=io; WHY="disk stalling everything (${iofull}% PSI io full)"
    fi
}

# ── the vote ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
vote_add() {
    hist="${hist}$1"
    while [ "${#hist}" -gt "$WINDOW" ]; do hist="${hist#?}"; done
}
vote_count() {
    local i n=0
    for ((i = 0; i < ${#hist}; i++)); do [ "${hist:i:1}" = "1" ] && n=$((n + 1)); done
    echo "$n"
}
vote_recent() {
    local tail3="${hist: -3}" i n=0
    for ((i = 0; i < ${#tail3}; i++)); do [ "${tail3:i:1}" = "1" ] && n=$((n + 1)); done
    echo "$n"
}

# The one place the ladder is written down, used by the loop and the selftest.
vote_fires() {
    [ "${#hist}" -ge "$WINDOW" ] \
        && [ "$(vote_count)" -ge "$HOT_MIN" ] \
        && [ "$(vote_recent)" -ge "$RECENT_MIN" ]
}

# ── one-shot report ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
report_sample() {
    local busy lag memfull iofull freq
    cpu_busy_now
    busy="$CPU_BUSY_NOW"
    lag=$(psi_some cpu); lag=${lag:-0}
    memfull=$(psi_full memory); memfull=${memfull:-0}
    iofull=$(psi_full io); iofull=${iofull:-0}
    freq=$(cur_freq_mhz)
    classify "$busy" "$lag" "$memfull" "$iofull" "$freq"
    echo "busy             : ${busy}%   (throttle branch needs >= ${WORK_BUSY}%)"
    echo "PSI cpu some     : ${lag}%   (stall line ${CPU_STALL}%, throttle floor ${CPU_STALL_FLOOR}%, needs ${ACTIVE_BUSY}% busy)"
    echo "PSI memory full  : ${memfull}%   (line ${MEM_FULL}%)"
    echo "PSI io full      : ${iofull}%   (line ${IO_FULL}%)"
    echo "fastest core     : ${freq} MHz (throttle line ${CLOCK_LINE} MHz)"
    echo "this sample      : $([ "$HOT" = "1" ] && echo "HOT — ${WHY}" || echo "cold — ${WHY}")"
    echo "owner's mode     : $(desired_mode)"
    echo "PPD profile      : $(powerprofilesctl get 2>/dev/null || echo '?')"
    echo "nvidia-powerd    : $(systemctl is-active nvidia-powerd.service 2>/dev/null || true)"
    echo "ladder           : ${HOT_MIN}/${WINDOW} hot, ${RECENT_MIN} of the last 3, ${INTERVAL}s apart, cooldown ${COOLDOWN}s"
    echo "auto-restore     : never — the boost stays until you pick a mode or run --release"
}

if [ "$STATUS_ONLY" = 1 ]; then
    echo "power-failsafe — $(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"
    sleep "$INTERVAL" 2>/dev/null || sleep 2
    report_sample
    exit 0
fi

# Ending a boost by hand, without having to pick a mode: the same code path the
# Eco stand-down uses, with the Eco rule bypassed.
if [ "$RELEASE_NOW" = 1 ]; then
    engaged=1
    engaged_mode=$(desired_mode)
    stand_down forced
    exit 0
fi

# ── the ladder, proven without a spun-up CPU ───────────────────────────────
# classify() and vote_fires() are the whole decision, so feeding them numbers
# tests the real thing. The cases are the calibrated ones from the header, plus
# the 2026-11-04 false raise (busy but capped, no stall) which must stay cold.
if [ "$SELFTEST" = 1 ]; then
    fails=0
    check() {  # name expected busy lag mem io freq
        classify "$3" "$4" "$5" "$6" "$7"
        if [ "$HOT" = "$2" ]; then
            printf '  ok    %-40s hot=%s  %s\n' "$1" "$HOT" "$WHY"
        else
            printf '  FAIL  %-40s expected hot=%s, got %s  %s\n' "$1" "$2" "$HOT" "$WHY"
            fails=$((fails + 1))
        fi
    }
    vcase() {  # name expected "history"
        hist="$3"
        if vote_fires; then fire=1; else fire=0; fi
        if [ "$fire" = "$2" ]; then
            printf '  ok    %-40s fires=%s  (%s/%s hot, %s last-3)\n' \
                "$1" "$fire" "$(vote_count)" "${#hist}" "$(vote_recent)"
        else
            printf '  FAIL  %-40s expected fires=%s, got %s  (%s/%s hot, %s last-3)\n' \
                "$1" "$2" "$fire" "$(vote_count)" "${#hist}" "$(vote_recent)"
            fails=$((fails + 1))
        fi
    }
    echo "sample classification (window ${WINDOW}, ${HOT_MIN}/${WINDOW} needed):"
    check "idle"                              0   2   0  0  4 3000
    check "busy at full clock, no stall"      0  99   3  0  4 3000
    check "busy but capped, PSI under floor"  0  96   5  0  4  900
    check "throttled hard with a hard stall"  1  96  20  0  4  800
    # The 08:24 raise this tuning exists for: throttled, but PSI only 8%.
    check "throttled, small stall (was a raise)" 0 99  8  0  4  800
    check "stalling hard at 60% busy"          1  60  30  0  4 2400
    # Used to be hot at 55% busy / 15% PSI; that is a loaded machine, not a
    # frozen one, and the owner felt every one of these as a mode change.
    check "mild stall at 55% busy"             0  55  15  0  4 2400
    check "PSI still decaying, machine idle"   0   4 40  0  4 2400
    check "memory thrash"                     1  20   2 12  4 3000
    check "disk stall"                        1  10   1  0 30 3000
    echo "vote:"
    vcase "one spike, rest cold"                0 "100000000000"
    vcase "window not full yet"                 0 "111"
    vcase "8 hot but the lag already stopped"   0 "111111110000"
    vcase "7 of 12 (the old 5-of-8 line)"       0 "110110101100"
    vcase "sustained with a hiccup"             1 "111110111111"
    vcase "jittery but sustained"               1 "101101110111"
    echo
    if [ "$fails" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "selftest: all checks passed"
        exit 0
    fi
    echo "selftest: ${fails} check(s) FAILED"
    exit 1
fi

log "watching for lag: stall >=${CPU_STALL}% PSI cpu at >=${ACTIVE_BUSY}% busy, OR throttled work (<=${CLOCK_LINE} MHz at >=${WORK_BUSY}% busy) with >=${CPU_STALL_FLOOR}% PSI, OR memory full >=${MEM_FULL}%, OR io full >=${IO_FULL}%"
log "ladder: ${HOT_MIN} of the last ${WINDOW} samples hot AND ${RECENT_MIN} of the last 3, ${INTERVAL}s apart, cooldown ${COOLDOWN}s"
if [ "${FAILSAFE_ESCALATE:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    if [ "${FAILSAFE_DRY_RUN:-0}" = "1" ]; then
        log "action: ESCALATE, DRY-RUN — decisions are logged, nothing is written"
    else
        log "action: ESCALATE — performance profile + GPU Dynamic Boost; NEVER backs out by itself: it stays until the mode is changed by hand or --release"
    fi
else
    log "action: ALARM ONLY — log, notify and blink; no profile, cap, turbo or GPU change (FAILSAFE_ESCALATE=1 opts in)"
fi

while true; do
    cpu_busy_now
    busy="$CPU_BUSY_NOW"
    lag=$(psi_some cpu); lag=${lag:-0}
    memfull=$(psi_full memory); memfull=${memfull:-0}
    iofull=$(psi_full io); iofull=${iofull:-0}
    freq=$(cur_freq_mhz)

    classify "$busy" "$lag" "$memfull" "$iofull" "$freq"
    vote_add "$HOT"

    if [ "$HOT" = "1" ]; then
        calm_run=0
        # Transitions only: the old daemon logged every sample and buried the
        # decision it was supposed to explain.
        if [ "$last_hot" != "1" ]; then
            log "lag sample: ${WHY} (${busy}% busy) — vote $(vote_count)/${WINDOW}"
        fi
    else
        calm_run=$((calm_run + 1))
        if [ "$last_hot" = "1" ]; then
            log "lag gone: ${WHY} (${busy}% busy)"
        fi
    fi
    last_hot="$HOT"

    # ── raise ──
    # The CURRENT sample must be hot as well as the vote: without that, a
    # window that filled during a stall and ended one sample later still fired,
    # which is raising Performance for load that has already stopped (measured
    # 2026-11-12 — the vote fired one tick after "lag gone").
    if [ "$engaged" != "1" ] && [ "$HOT" = "1" ] && vote_fires; then
        now=$(date +%s)
        if [ $((now - last_action)) -ge "$COOLDOWN" ]; then
            # ═══ A MEMORY OR DISK STALL IS NOT A POWER PROBLEM (2026-11-17) ══
            #
            # This used to raise the performance profile for ANY lane, and the
            # cost of that was the freeze this whole schedule came from:
            #
            #   07:48:57  lag sample: memory reclaim stalling everything (8% PSI mem full) (8% busy)
            #   07:49:06  cpu profile -> performance
            #   07:49:07  [converge] refresh 2560x1600@120.00000 -> 2560x1600@240.00000
            #
            # The escalation runs power-autoswitch --converge, and that moves
            # the panel refresh with the profile (the owner's rule, and it is
            # right for a power decision). A refresh change is a MODESET: the
            # compositor reconfigures the output, and measured on this machine
            # it blocks the event loop for ~600 ms with the screen frozen and
            # every client repainting afterwards -- "my PC freezes for a few
            # seconds when I switch tabs". Reclaim was already handling the
            # stall; the profile change could not fix it and cost seconds.
            #
            # The header always said memory/io/GPU are "ignored outright".
            # They were not: they were classified, and then they climbed the
            # same ladder as a pegged CPU. Now the lane decides. CPU lanes still
            # raise what the owner asked for; the other two report and leave the
            # machine alone, so nothing here can modeset the screen.
            if [ "$LANE" != "cpu" ]; then
                log "lag ${WHY} — a ${LANE} stall, not a CPU one: reporting only, profile and refresh left alone"
                notify -i "$HYPR_ICON" "Stalling, but not the CPU" \
                    "${WHY}. Nothing was changed — a CPU profile cannot fix ${LANE} reclaim."
                last_action=$(date +%s)
                hist=""
                cool_log_at=0
            else
            apply_performance "$(vote_count)/${WINDOW}" "$busy" "$WHY"
            cool_log_at=0
            # A fresh window after a decision: a long stall does not re-fire
            # the moment the cooldown ends, it has to build the vote again.
            hist=""
            fi
        elif [ $((now - ${cool_log_at:-0})) -ge 30 ]; then
            # Once per cooldown, not once per tick: a sustained stall used to
            # write this line twelve times into the journal.
            log "vote $(vote_count)/${WINDOW} says lag, but cooling down ($((COOLDOWN - (now - last_action)))s left) — no action"
            cool_log_at=$now
        fi
    fi

    # ── and while we hold a boost, whose is it? ──
    if [ "$engaged" = "1" ]; then
        owner_mode=$(desired_mode)
        profile=$(powerprofilesctl get 2>/dev/null || echo "?")
        if [ -n "${engaged_mode:-}" ] && [ "$owner_mode" != "$engaged_mode" ]; then
            # The owner picked a mode while we held the boost. That switch is
            # theirs: no stand-down, no re-raise, no fight — exactly the
            # "don't switch it while I'm switching it" rule.
            log "owner switched the mode while the boost was held (${engaged_mode} -> ${owner_mode}) — standing off, leaving it alone"
            engaged=0; engaged_mode=""; hist=""; calm_run=0
            last_action=$(date +%s)
        elif [ "$profile" != "performance" ]; then
            log "cpu profile is '${profile}', not the performance this raised — nothing of ours left to undo"
            engaged=0; engaged_mode=""; hist=""
        # NO stand-down on calm (2026-11-21). The boost stays until the owner
        # picks a mode or runs --release; backing out to Eco by itself was the
        # "it keeps switching itself back" the owner asked to stop. A manual
        # Eco still ends it through the profile branch above, which sees the
        # profile leave performance and drops our claim without a fight.
        fi
    fi
    sleep "$INTERVAL"
done
